/* ==============================================================
   03 — Pick and place: the full demo
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   Everything above, assembled into one cycle
   NEEDS     Robot activated. Tool optional — see TOOL below.
   WATCH     A four-part tray cycle that looks like a product
   TIME      ~30 s for the full cycle
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   WHAT THIS IS

   The demo to run when someone says "show me what it does". It
   picks four parts from a row and places them into a 2x2 tray,
   turning each one a quarter turn on the way, and it is built
   entirely from the pieces in basic/ and intermediate/ — no new
   commands.

   Read it as a worked example of how the pieces fit:

     WRF          all coordinates are relative to the fixture,
                  so moving the fixture is a one-line change
     TRF          poses refer to the tool tip, 30 mm below the
                  shaft
     SetConf      posture pinned, so every cycle is identical
     SetConfTurn  turn pinned, so a cabled tool comes back the
                  same way round every cycle
     MoveJump     every transfer: lift, travel, land, in one
                  command each
     Blending     off throughout, because MoveJump requires it —
                  the jumps are smooth on their own
     Checkpoints  so a host knows where the robot actually is

   Compare it with the Meca500 version of this demo, if you have
   seen it: each pick there is six lines of MovePose, MoveLin and
   relative moves. Here it is one MoveJump. That is the SCARA.

   THE STRUCTURE

   Notice what this file does NOT have: a loop. Four picks means
   four blocks, written out. That is the honest consequence of a
   robot with no flow control, and it is worth showing rather
   than hiding — then explain that in production the host issues
   StartProgram("cell/pick") four times with a different variable
   each time, and the repetition disappears. See 01_variables and
   02_program_calls.

   TOOL: WHAT THIS DEMO LEAVES OUT

   The MCS500 has no tool I/O of its own: the manual says
   end-of-arm tooling is controlled directly from your PLC. So
   this program cannot close a gripper or switch on a vacuum —
   and it should not try. At each pick and each place it stops,
   reports a checkpoint, and waits for a moment. That pause is a
   stand-in: it is where the host would work the tool.

   Which also means this program assumes every grip works. A real
   cell checks: switch the tool, ask whether a part is actually
   held, and only then carry on; if it is not, release, lift, go
   home, report the error and stop. The robot has no IF, so that
   check lives in the host — and the program has to be split at
   the grip to give the host the chance to make it.

   The demo stays in one piece because it has to run from the
   MecaPortal with nothing else connected. Its checkpoints 11, 21,
   31, 41 sit exactly where a host would be listening; what is
   missing is the other half of the conversation. IN A REAL CELL,
   at the bottom of this file, puts it back. Run this one; build
   that one.

   GEOMETRY

   Parts come from a row of four, 25 mm apart, and go into a 2x2
   tray 30 mm apart. Both are defined relative to the WRF, whose
   origin is on the fixture's top surface, so if you have a real
   fixture, measure it once and change the single SetWrf line.
   Every pick and place height is 10 mm above that surface — the
   top of a 10 mm part.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* ============ SETUP ========================================== */

/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


/* Start from a known state. Everything this program does not
   explicitly set — payload, torque limits, move mode, joint
   velocity limit — comes from here rather than from whatever ran
   before it.

   Save 00_common/01_init.mxprog on the robot as
   "00_common/01_init" first, or this reports [3017]. If you have
   not, comment the line out and uncomment the fallback block
   below it. */
StartProgram("00_common/01_init")

/* --- Overrides for THIS program ------------------------------
   Everything below deliberately overrides what 00_common/01_init
   set. That is the pattern: init establishes the house defaults,
   the program states its own needs, and nothing is inherited by
   accident. */

// Speed. MoveJump is a joint-space move, so this one number is
// the whole tuning conversation for this program.
SetJointVel(30)

// Pin the arm posture and the shaft's turn so every cycle is
// identical. 00_common/01_init leaves automatic selection on,
// which is the safe general default; a production cycle wants
// both pinned. Righty, normal turn range.
SetConf(1)
SetConfTurn(0)

// The tool: the house tool from intermediate/02, its tip 30 mm
// below the end of the shaft. Put in your real length.
SetTrf(0, 0, -30, 0)

// The fixture. Everything below is measured from here, so this
// is the only line that changes when the fixture moves: its top
// surface, 150 mm in front of the robot and 20 mm above the
// bottom of the base.
SetWrf(150, 0, 20, 0)

// MoveJump needs blending off between jumps.
SetBlending(0)

// The shape of every hop: lift and land through 20 mm of
// straight vertical, and peak 25 to 35 mm above the higher end —
// clear of the parts still in the row.
SetMoveJumpHeight(20, 20, 25, 35)

/* --- Fallback, if 00_common/01_init is not saved on the robot -
   Uncomment these if you commented out the StartProgram above. */
// SetMoveMode(0)
// SetJointVelLimit(100)
// SetMoveJumpApproachVel(10, 1, 10, 1)
// SetPayload(0, 0, 0, 0)
// SetTorqueLimits(100, 100, 100, 100)


/* ============ HOME ========================================== */

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)
MovePose(0, 0, 20, 0)                  // hover above the fixture
SetCheckpoint(1)                       // "ready, tray may be loaded"


/* ============ PART 1 ======================================== */
/* Pick from row position 1, place into tray cell 1.

   Read this block carefully — the three below are identical
   except for the two coordinate lines, and that repetition is
   the point being made in the header. */

// -- pick --
MoveJump(-40, -50, 10, 0)              // hop onto the part
SetCheckpoint(11)                      // "at the part" — NOT
                                       // proof that a part is held
Delay(0.3)                             // TOOL: the host grips here
// ^ In a real cell the program ENDS here. The host switches the
//   tool, asks whether it is holding a part, then starts either
//   the place program or the abort program. This demo cannot
//   ask, so it carries on regardless. See IN A REAL CELL, below.

// -- place --
MoveJump(-10, 30, 10, 90)              // hop to the tray cell,
                                       // turning a quarter turn
SetCheckpoint(12)                      // "part 1 at the tray"
Delay(0.3)                             // TOOL: the host releases here


/* ============ PART 2 ======================================== */

MoveJump(-15, -50, 10, 0)
SetCheckpoint(21)
Delay(0.3)

MoveJump(20, 30, 10, 90)
SetCheckpoint(22)
Delay(0.3)


/* ============ PART 3 ======================================== */

MoveJump(10, -50, 10, 0)
SetCheckpoint(31)
Delay(0.3)

MoveJump(-10, 60, 10, 90)
SetCheckpoint(32)
Delay(0.3)


/* ============ PART 4 ======================================== */

MoveJump(35, -50, 10, 0)
SetCheckpoint(41)
Delay(0.3)

MoveJump(20, 60, 10, 90)
SetCheckpoint(42)
Delay(0.3)


/* ============ FINISH ======================================== */

MoveJump(0, 0, 20, 0)                  // back to the hover pose
SetCheckpoint(99)                      // "cycle complete"

// Put everything back to defaults so the next example starts
// from a known state.
SetWrf(0, 0, 0, 0)
SetTrf(0, 0, 0, 0)
SetAutoConf(1)
SetAutoConfTurn(1)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)
SetBlending(100)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   IN A REAL CELL: THE GRIP HANDSHAKE

   Everything above runs open loop. This is what a production
   version of the same cycle does differently, and why.

   THE HANDSHAKE

       switch the tool on
       ask: is it holding a part?
         yes   carry on to the tray
         no    release, lift, go home, report the error, stop

   The robot cannot run this — there is no IF, and on the MCS500
   it does not even own the tool. And it cannot be bolted onto the
   program above either: by the time the tool has switched, the
   jump to the tray is already queued behind it. The decision has
   to be made BEFORE the next motion is queued, not while it is
   running.

   So the program is split at the grip. The pick program ends
   with the tool down at the part. With nothing left in its
   queue, the arm simply stays there. That pause is the host's
   window to act and decide.

   THE ROBOT PROGRAMS

   Three programs, saved on the robot (see 02_program_calls):

     cell/pick_1
         MoveJump(-40, -50, 10, 0)
         SetCheckpoint(11)            tool down at the part

     cell/place_1
         MoveJump(-10, 30, 10, 90)
         SetCheckpoint(12)            tool down at the tray cell

     cell/abort
         MoveJump(0, 0, 20, 0)        straight up, then home
         MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)
         SetCheckpoint(90)            "aborted, arm clear"

   All three rely on the SETUP section of this file having run
   first — the frames above all. Settings are sticky, so a cell
   runs its setup once, then calls these as often as it likes.

   The Delays are gone. They were a guess at how long the tool
   takes; the host now waits for the tool to say so.

   One abort program serves all four pick points, because a
   MoveJump always lifts straight up first, wherever the tool
   happens to be — it cannot drag sideways through the fixture.
   Note the order: the host releases the tool BEFORE starting the
   abort. That leaves a half-gripped part where it is, rather
   than carrying something nobody is sure of.

   THE HOST SIDE

       StartProgram("cell/pick_1")
       wait for [3030][11]           tool down at the part
       switch the tool on            the host's own output
       wait for "part held"          the tool's own feedback,
         with a timeout              read by the host
       decide:
         held        StartProgram("cell/place_1")
                     wait for [3030][12], release the tool,
                     confirm it let go, carry on
         missed      release the tool
                     StartProgram("cell/abort")
                     wait for [3030][90]
                     raise the alarm, stop the cycle
         timed out   a fault, not a miss: raise the alarm, and
                     leave the arm where it is until someone
                     has looked

   Wait for the checkpoint FIRST. Before it arrives the tool may
   not be at the part yet, and anything you switch or read is
   early.

   If [3040][11] arrives instead of [3030][11], the checkpoint was
   discarded — the robot stopped in error or on a safety signal —
   and the cycle is already over. That is the host's general
   error handling, not this handshake.

   What the operator does next — retry, skip that position, stop
   the batch — is a host decision, made on the HMI. The robot
   programs do not need to know.

   WHERE "PART HELD" COMES FROM

   From the tool, through the host's own I/O: an electric
   gripper's "holding" signal, a vacuum switch on the suction
   line, a presence sensor in the jaws. Whatever it is, it is
   wired to the host, because the host is the one that has to
   decide.

   THE OTHER HANDSHAKES

   The grip is the one that matters most, but a real cell has a
   few more of the same shape:

     before the pick     is there a part? A sensor or a camera,
                         read by the host.
     before the place    still holding? Parts get lost in
                         transit. Same check, same split.
     before the place    is the tray cell empty?
     after the release   did it let go?

   WHAT IT COSTS

   Every split is a stop and a round trip to the host. That is
   cheap next to a dropped part, but it is not free, and on a
   SCARA — where cycle time is the reason it was bought — it
   shows. Put decision points where a wrong answer costs
   something. The grip always qualifies.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Start at SetJointVel(30). Run it once at that speed so
      people can follow what is happening.

   2. Run it again at SetJointVel(100). Same program, one edited
      number. This is the moment the cycle time argument gets
      made, and it makes itself.

   3. Have the log panel open. The checkpoint stream — 11, 12,
      21, 22, ... 99 — is what a PLC integrator will actually be
      looking at, and seeing it live is more useful to them than
      watching the arm.

   4. Change the single SetWrf line and run again. The whole
      cycle relocates. If you have a fixture you can physically
      move, this is the strongest thirty seconds in the demo.

   QUESTIONS THIS WILL PROVOKE

   "Why is every part written out four times?"
      Because the robot has no loops. In production the host
      calls one saved program four times with different
      variables. Show advanced/01 and advanced/02.

   "What if the part isn't there?"
      The host knows, from the tool's own feedback or a sensor,
      and decides: carry on, or abort, go home and raise the
      alarm. See IN A REAL CELL. Whether a part is waiting to be
      picked in the first place is a sensor or camera question —
      also the host's.

   "How do I switch the tool from the robot program?"
      You do not, on the MCS500: the tool is wired to the PLC.
      That sounds like a limitation until you notice the PLC is
      already the one deciding whether the grip worked.

   "How accurate is the placement?"
      Every MoveJump lands on its target and stops there — there
      is no blending at the work points. Repeatability is a
      datasheet number — quote the datasheet, not the demo.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
